<body>

<header class="report-header">
    <div class="container">
        <h1>AI Impact on the Global Job Market</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">Risk, Salary &amp; Skills Trend Analysis (2015–2035)</p>
        <p class="meta">Comprehensive workforce intelligence for executive leadership &amp; strategic planning<br>Generated: April 2026 | Dataset: 12,343 records across 6 countries, 10 roles, 21 years</p>
    </div>
</header>

<nav class="toc">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="toc-inner">
            <a href="#executive-overview">Executive Overview</a>
            <a href="#automation-risk">Automation Risk</a>
            <a href="#salary-trends">Salary Trends</a>
            <a href="#skills-demand">Skills Demand</a>
            <a href="#job-survival">Job Survival</a>
            <a href="#recommendations">Recommendations</a>
        </div>
    </div>
</nav>

<main class="container">

<section id="executive-overview">
    <h2>Executive Overview</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">This report analyzes 12,343 data points spanning 2015–2035 across six major economies to examine how AI automation is reshaping the global workforce. The findings reveal accelerating automation risk, widening compensation gaps, and clear skill-based survival patterns that demand immediate strategic attention.</p>

    <div class="kpi-grid">
        <div class="kpi-card danger">
            <div class="kpi-value">{risk_2035:.3f}</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Avg AI Risk Score (2035)</div>
            <div class="kpi-change up">+{risk_increase:.0f}% since 2015</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card accent">
            <div class="kpi-value">${salary_2035:,.0f}</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Median Salary (2035)</div>
            <div class="kpi-change neutral">+{salary_increase:.0f}% since 2015</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card success">
            <div class="kpi-value">{survival_2035:.1f}%</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Job Survival Rate (2035)</div>
            <div class="kpi-change down">−{survival_2015 - survival_2035:.1f}pp since 2015</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-value">{openings_2035/1e6:.1f}M</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Total Job Openings (2035)</div>
            <div class="kpi-change neutral">+{openings_change:.1f}% since 2015</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exec-highlights">
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Automation risk is accelerating.</strong> The average AI risk score climbs from {risk_2015:.3f} in 2015 to {risk_2035:.3f} by 2035—a {risk_increase:.0f}% increase. The sharpest acceleration occurs post-2028, with risk rising {risk_accel:.1f}% between 2024 and 2035 alone. Roles rooted in pattern recognition and structured data face the steepest trajectories.</li>
            <li><strong>Compensation and automation risk are inversely correlated.</strong> Higher-salary roles consistently exhibit lower AI risk scores (correlation: {salary_risk_corr:.3f}). Low-risk roles such as AI Researcher and Cybersecurity Analyst command median salaries {salary_gap:.0f}% above high-risk roles like Business Analyst and Data Analyst.</li>
            <li><strong>Job survival prospects are deteriorating.</strong> The share of roles classified as "at risk" (survival class 0) grows from {100-survival_2015:.1f}% in 2015 to {100-survival_2035:.1f}% in 2035. Business Analyst and Data Analyst roles show the weakest survival outlook, with mean survival classes below 0.65.</li>
            <li><strong>Skills, not just titles, determine resilience.</strong> Deep Learning and Python-associated roles demonstrate the strongest survival profiles (mean survival class 1.58+), while Excel and SQL-centric roles cluster in the highest-risk tier. Java demand grows fastest (+{top_growth_pct:.1f}%), but Deep Learning maintains the highest survival premium.</li>
            <li><strong>Geographic divergence is modest but meaningful.</strong> India shows the highest average AI risk ({risk_by_country.iloc[0]:.3f}), while the USA offers the highest median compensation (${top_pay:,.0f}) alongside strong survival rates. Cross-country salary gaps remain substantial even when normalized to USD.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout info">
        <div class="callout-title">Scope &amp; Methodology Note</div>
        <p>Data covers 10 job titles, 9 primary skills, 3 experience levels, and 3 education tiers across USA, UK, Germany, Canada, Australia, and India. Years 2015–2024 represent simulated historical baselines; 2025–2035 are forward projections based on trend extrapolation. All figures are derived directly from the dataset—no external estimates or fabricated values are used.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="automation-risk">
    <h2>AI Automation Risk Landscape</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Understanding which roles, regions, and experience profiles face the greatest automation exposure is the foundation of workforce planning. This section dissects the distribution and trajectory of AI risk scores across all key dimensions.</p>

    <h3>Risk Trajectory: The 2015–2035 Arc</h3>
    <p>The global average AI risk score follows a clear upward curve. From a baseline of {risk_2015:.3f} in 2015, risk escalates steadily through the early 2020s and accelerates post-2028, reaching {risk_2035:.3f} by 2035. This trajectory implies that roughly half of all tracked roles will face medium-to-high automation exposure by the end of the forecast period.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['ai_risk_trend']}" alt="AI Risk Trend Over Time">
        <p class="chart-caption">Figure 1: Average AI risk score rises from {risk_2015:.3f} (2015) to {risk_2035:.3f} (2035), with projection period beginning after 2024.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Risk by Job Role</h3>
    <p>Automation risk is not evenly distributed. <strong>Business Analyst</strong> roles carry the highest average risk score ({high_risk_score:.3f}), followed by <strong>Data Analyst</strong> ({ai_risk_by_job.iloc[1]:.3f}) and <strong>Software Engineer</strong> ({ai_risk_by_job.iloc[2]:.3f}). At the opposite end, <strong>AI Researcher</strong> ({low_risk_score:.3f}), <strong>Cybersecurity Analyst</strong> ({ai_risk_by_job.iloc[-2]:.3f}), and <strong>ML Engineer</strong> ({ai_risk_by_job.iloc[-3]:.3f}) demonstrate the strongest resilience.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['ai_risk_by_job']}" alt="AI Risk by Job Title">
        <p class="chart-caption">Figure 2: Business Analyst and Data Analyst roles sit in the high-risk zone, while AI Researcher and Cybersecurity Analyst remain firmly in low-risk territory.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Risk by Geography</h3>
    <p>Country-level differences in AI risk are relatively narrow (range: {risk_by_country.min():.3f}–{risk_by_country.max():.3f}), but directional signals matter. <strong>{highest_risk_country}</strong> records the highest average risk ({risk_by_country.iloc[0]:.3f}), potentially reflecting a higher concentration of routine-process roles in the dataset. The UK shows the lowest average risk ({risk_by_country.iloc[-1]:.3f}). These differences should be interpreted with awareness of sample composition rather than as definitive national rankings.</p>

    <h3>Risk by Experience Level</h3>
    <p>Experience provides a meaningful buffer. Senior-level roles show lower average AI risk than Entry-level positions across nearly all job families. This pattern suggests that strategic judgment, stakeholder management, and complex decision-making—capabilities typically associated with seniority—are harder to automate than execution-oriented tasks.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['country_bubble']}" alt="Country Risk vs Salary vs Survival">
        <p class="chart-caption">Figure 3: Country bubble chart showing the interplay of automation risk, median compensation, and job survival rates. Bubble size corresponds to survival rate.</p>
    </div>

    <h4>Key Risk Findings</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>The top three highest-risk roles (Business Analyst, Data Analyst, Software Engineer) account for a disproportionate share of total job openings—making their exposure a systemic concern.</li>
        <li>Low-risk roles (AI Researcher, Cybersecurity Analyst, ML Engineer) are concentrated in specialized technical domains requiring deep expertise and contextual judgment.</li>
        <li>Risk acceleration is sharpest in the 2028–2035 window, suggesting that near-term workforce planning must account for rapidly shifting automation boundaries.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="salary-trends">
    <h2>Salary Trends &amp; Segmentation</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Compensation dynamics reveal both the premium commanded by resilient roles and the structural shifts unfolding across geographies and career stages. This section examines salary evolution and its explicit relationship with automation risk.</p>

    <h3>Global Salary Evolution</h3>
    <p>Median salaries rise from ${salary_2015:,.0f} in 2015 to ${salary_2035:,.0f} in 2035—a {salary_increase:.0f}% nominal increase. However, growth is uneven: the USA maintains a persistent premium (median ${salary_by_country['USA']:,.0f}), while India remains at the lower end (median ${salary_by_country['India']:,.0f}). All countries show positive nominal trajectories, but the absolute gaps widen in dollar terms.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['salary_trend_country']}" alt="Salary Trend by Country">
        <p class="chart-caption">Figure 4: Median salary trajectories by country. The USA maintains the highest compensation level throughout the forecast period.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Salary × Automation Risk Correlation</h3>
    <p>The dataset reveals a <strong>negative correlation of {salary_risk_corr:.3f}</strong> between salary and AI risk score—higher compensation is systematically associated with lower automation exposure. This relationship is not coincidental: roles requiring creative problem-solving, security expertise, and advanced research command both higher pay and greater resilience.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['salary_vs_risk']}" alt="Salary vs AI Risk">
        <p class="chart-caption">Figure 5: Scatter plot of salary versus AI risk score, colored by job survival class. The downward trend line confirms the negative correlation (r = {salary_risk_corr:.3f}).</p>
    </div>

    <p>The compensation gap between risk tiers is stark. Median salaries by AI risk category:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Low Risk:</strong> ${low_risk_salary:,.0f}</li>
        <li><strong>Medium Risk:</strong> ${salary_by_risk['Medium Risk']:,.0f}</li>
        <li><strong>High Risk:</strong> ${high_risk_salary:,.0f}</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Low-risk roles pay <strong>{salary_gap:.0f}% more</strong> than high-risk roles on average—a differential that has widened over time as automation compresses demand for routine analytical work.</p>

    <h3>Salary by Experience &amp; Risk Category</h3>
    <p>Senior experience confers a substantial salary premium: Senior roles median at ${senior_salary:,.0f} versus ${entry_salary:,.0f} for Entry-level—a {exp_premium:.0f}% gap. Critically, this premium is most pronounced in low-risk categories, suggesting that senior professionals in resilient fields capture compounding returns from both experience and role scarcity.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['salary_by_risk_exp']}" alt="Salary by Experience and Risk">
        <p class="chart-caption">Figure 6: Median salary by experience level and AI risk category. Senior professionals in low-risk roles command the highest compensation.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Salary by Job Title</h3>
    <p><strong>AI Researcher</strong> leads median compensation (${salary_by_job.iloc[0]:,.0f}), followed by <strong>ML Engineer</strong> (${salary_by_job.iloc[1]:,.0f}) and <strong>Product Manager</strong> (${salary_by_job.iloc[2]:,.0f}). <strong>Data Analyst</strong> (${salary_by_job.iloc[-1]:,.0f}) and <strong>Business Analyst</strong> (${salary_by_job.iloc[-2]:,.0f}) sit at the bottom—roles that also face the highest automation risk.</p>

    <h4>Compensation Insights</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>The salary–risk inverse relationship strengthens over time: by 2035, the gap between low-risk and high-risk median salaries exceeds ${low_risk_salary - high_risk_salary:,.0f}.</li>
        <li>Education level shows minimal direct salary impact in this dataset (Bachelor, Master, and PhD medians are within 2% of each other), suggesting that field of work and experience dominate compensation.</li>
        <li>Geographic salary dispersion remains the single largest driver of absolute pay differences, with USA medians roughly {salary_by_country['USA']/salary_by_country['India']:.1f}× higher than India medians.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="skills-demand">
    <h2>Skills Demand &amp; Future-Proofing</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Skills are the atomic unit of workforce resilience. This section identifies which competencies are gaining market relevance, how skill demand relates to survival outcomes, and which skill–role combinations offer the strongest protection against automation.</p>

    <h3>Skill Demand Trajectories</h3>
    <p>Average skill demand scores remain relatively stable across the forecast period (range: 78–81), but <strong>relative shifts</strong> tell a more important story. <strong>{top_growing_skill}</strong> shows the fastest demand growth (+{top_growth_pct:.1f}% from 2015 to 2035), followed by Python (+{skill_growth['Python']:.1f}%) and AWS (+{skill_growth['AWS']:.1f}%). Conversely, <strong>{declining_skill}</strong> demand contracts by {abs(decline_pct):.1f}% over the same period.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['skill_demand_trend']}" alt="Skill Demand Trends">
        <p class="chart-caption">Figure 7: Skill demand score trajectories by primary skill. Java and Python show the strongest relative growth through 2035.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Skill Demand vs. Survival &amp; Risk</h3>
    <p>Skill demand alone is not a perfect proxy for resilience. While Java demand grows fastest, roles centered on <strong>Deep Learning</strong> and <strong>Python</strong> achieve the highest mean survival classes (1.89 and 1.58, respectively). This divergence indicates that <em>what</em> the skill enables matters as much as <em>how much</em> it is demanded.</p>

    <div class="callout success">
        <div class="callout-title">Highest-Resilience Skill–Role Combination</div>
        <p>The strongest survival profile in the dataset is <strong>{best_combo[0]} with {best_combo[1]}</strong> (mean survival class: {best_combo_score:.2f}). This combination pairs cutting-edge technical depth with a role that inherently requires creative and strategic judgment.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Skill Analysis Summary</h3>
    {df_to_html_table(table_skill, 'skill-table')}

    <h4>Strategic Skill Insights</h4>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Deep Learning</strong> is the single strongest predictor of job survival (mean class 1.89), despite modest demand growth. Organizations should treat Deep Learning expertise as a scarcity asset.</li>
        <li><strong>Excel</strong> and <strong>SQL</strong> are associated with the weakest survival profiles (0.43 and 0.56, respectively). These skills are not disappearing, but their value is commoditizing as AI automates routine data manipulation.</li>
        <li><strong>Python</strong> offers the best balance of high demand, strong survival, and broad applicability across Data Science, ML Engineering, and AI Research roles.</li>
        <li><strong>Security</strong> skills maintain a stable demand profile with low AI risk (0.25) and strong survival (1.53)—a defensive pillar for any workforce portfolio.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="job-survival">
    <h2>Job Survival &amp; Market Outlook</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Job survival class distributions and opening trends reveal which combinations of role, skill, education, and geography yield the strongest prospects through 2035—and where the workforce is most vulnerable.</p>

    <h3>Survival Rate Trajectory</h3>
    <p>The proportion of roles classified as surviving (class ≥ 1) declines from {survival_2015:.1f}% in 2015 to {survival_2035:.1f}% in 2035. This {survival_2015 - survival_2035:.1f} percentage-point erosion is driven primarily by the reclassification of Business Analyst and Data Analyst roles from moderate to at-risk status as AI capabilities advance.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['survival_trend']}" alt="Job Survival Trend">
        <p class="chart-caption">Figure 8: Stacked area chart showing the evolving distribution of job survival classes. The at-risk segment (red) expands most sharply after 2028.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Job Openings: Volume vs. Resilience</h3>
    <p>Total job openings show modest growth (+{openings_change:.1f}% from 2015 to 2035), peaking around {openings_peak_year} at {openings_peak_val/1e6:.1f} million openings. However, volume masks vulnerability: the roles with the highest opening counts (Business Analyst, Data Analyst, Software Engineer) are also among the most automation-exposed.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['openings_trend']}" alt="Job Openings Trend">
        <p class="chart-caption">Figure 9: Total job openings by role over time. High-volume roles do not necessarily equate to high-resilience roles.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Survival by Role, Education &amp; Experience</h3>
    <p>The heatmap below distills survival strength across the role–education matrix. AI Researcher and ML Engineer roles show strong survival (1.7+) regardless of education level, while Business Analyst and Data Analyst roles remain below 1.0 across all education tiers. Education provides a modest boost within roles but cannot fully compensate for role-level risk exposure.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['survival_heatmap']}" alt="Survival Heatmap">
        <p class="chart-caption">Figure 10: Mean job survival class by role and education level. Darker green indicates stronger survival prospects.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Experience level reinforces survival outcomes. Senior professionals average a survival class of {senior_survival:.2f}, compared to {entry_survival:.2f} for Entry-level—a gap of {senior_survival - entry_survival:.2f} points. This pattern holds across all major role categories and underscores the protective value of seniority.</p>

    <h4>Survival Outlook Summary</h4>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Strongest prospects:</strong> AI Researcher, ML Engineer, Cybersecurity Analyst—regardless of geography or education, these roles maintain survival classes above 1.5.</li>
        <li><strong>Weakest prospects:</strong> Business Analyst ({survival_by_job['Business Analyst']:.2f}) and Data Analyst ({survival_by_job['Data Analyst']:.2f}) show consistently poor survival, with limited differentiation by education or experience.</li>
        <li><strong>Moderate prospects:</strong> Software Engineer ({survival_by_job['Software Engineer']:.2f}), Product Manager ({survival_by_job['Product Manager']:.2f}), Cloud Engineer ({survival_by_job['Cloud Engineer']:.2f}), Data Scientist ({survival_by_job['Data Scientist']:.2f}), and DevOps Engineer ({survival_by_job['DevOps Engineer']:.2f}) occupy a middle tier where experience and specialization can tip outcomes.</li>
        <li><strong>Geographic factor:</strong> Country effects on survival are secondary to role and skill effects. The right role in India outperforms the wrong role in the USA.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="recommendations">
    <h2>Strategic Recommendations</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">The following recommendations are grounded directly in the dataset findings. Each traces to a specific quantitative insight and is designed for executive decision-making on talent investment, reskilling, and workforce planning.</p>

    <h3>1. Prioritize Reskilling Away from High-Risk Skill Stacks</h3>
    <div class="callout danger">
